### D-34
date: 2026-10-14
milestone: M7
scope: publishing
title: Usuals is live without OAuth verification; verification is submitted next, with scope justifications and a silent demo video
question: D-31 recorded that the author believed the OAuth consent screen was already verified. Is it, and what does the store listing being live mean for sign-in?
answer: "Not verified (found 2026-10-14). The Chrome Web Store review and Google's OAuth verification are separate: the store approved and published the item, but signing in with a fresh account shows Google's unverified-app warning, and Google Auth Platform → Data Access reports \"Missing the following fields for one or more requested scopes: scope justification, demo video\". Both requested scopes (`calendar.events.owned`, `calendar.calendarlist.readonly`) are sensitive, so until verification passes every new user sees the warning and the app is capped at 100 users. me@kfireliyahu.com is not a fair test of what a user sees: it had already granted access, and it belongs to the project's own organization.\n\nWhat goes in, as M7 U11: (1) a justification per scope, drafted from what src/background.ts calls: calendarList.list only, to show the user's calendars as groups and to choose one; events.insert for each drop or Schedule, into the calendar the user chose, and events.delete only for Undo of that same event; nothing else is read, changed or deleted. (2) A demo video, YouTube Unlisted (not Private), silent with one caption per shot (narration is not required), recorded with a new Gmail account made for it in a fresh Chrome profile signed in to Chrome as that account (getAuthToken uses the profile's account), with the demo .ics files imported and the account's language English. It shows the client ID (chrome-extension://hkihoifbohighgoackpcinobeabpjnbp/manifest.json and the Credentials page), the full consent flow with the app name Usuals, the calendar list in use, a drop creating an event and Undo deleting it. Whether getAuthToken's popup shows the URL with client_id is not known; if not, the submission notes say so and point at the timestamp where the ID is shown."
supersedes: D-31
state: amended

### D-35
date: 2026-10-17
milestone: M7
scope: publishing
title: Google approved Usuals' OAuth verification; the unverified-app warning is gone
question: D-34 left Usuals live without OAuth verification. Where does it stand now?
answer: "Approved. The author submitted verification on 2026-10-15 (M7 U11) and Google's Third Party Data Safety Team approved it by 2026-10-17 for project 85811622726 (calendar-usuals). The approval email names one scope, `calendar.events.owned`; `calendar.calendarlist.readonly` is not named, and whether it needed approval at all (it may count as non-sensitive) was not checked: Verification Center or Data Access says. Per the email, a new scope or any change to the consent screen's configuration needs a new verification request, so the scopes and branding stay as they are unless that is the point.\n\nA fresh install and sign-in on 2026-10-17 went smoothly, with one dialog before it: Chrome's \"Proceed with caution: this extension is not trusted by Enhanced Safe Browsing\", which users with Enhanced Safe Browsing see for extensions from publishers new to the store. It is not about OAuth and nothing in the code changes it; Google trusts a publisher once it has followed the Developer Program Policies for some months.\n\nStill to check: the consent screen's publishing status (Google Auth Platform → Audience). An earlier note recorded it as External, in Testing, test user me@; in Testing only listed test users can sign in, which would explain a friend of the author being shown a request-access screen on 2026-10-16. If it says Testing, switch it to In production. The other explanation for that screen is a work or school account whose admin restricts apps (chrome.identity signs in with the Chrome profile's account).\n\nThe store showed 4 users on 2026-10-17. The store counts installed Chrome profiles that check for updates, not Google accounts, so the author's profiles and the friend's install each count, sign-in or not; unpacked builds do not count. Who the fourth is was not worked out."
supersedes: D-34
state: live

### D-36
date: 2026-10-17
milestone: M8
scope: extension
title: Sign-in tries Chrome's getAuthToken first and falls back to launchWebAuthFlow with a Web application client
question: How does Usuals sign in where chrome.identity.getAuthToken does not work (Brave, Edge, Opera, Arc)?
answer: getAuthToken stays the first path, so Chrome behaves as before. When it fails (except a Chrome user closing the consent window), an implicit-grant launchWebAuthFlow with a separate Web application OAuth client takes over, its token cached with its expiry in chrome.storage.session and renewed silently with prompt=none; the working path is remembered in chrome.storage.local. The fallback is off until WEB_AUTH_CLIENT_ID is filled in.
state: live

### D-37
date: 2026-10-17
milestone: M8
scope: extension
title: A press with Shift already down is followed with mouse events, since Chrome starts no drag from it
question: Why did holding Shift before dragging an item create a single event, and how is it fixed?
answer: Chrome starts no HTML drag from a Shift-press (Blink treats it as extending the selection; only links and images are exempt), so no dragstart fired. The row now hands a Shift-press to content.tsx, which follows the pointer with mousemove/mouseup, shows the same tooltip and preview, swallows the release on the grid and creates the event there, weekly while Shift is held. The drag tooltip also says 'Hold Shift to repeat weekly' from the start of every drag.
state: live

### D-4
date: 2026-09-06
milestone: M2
scope: extension
title: The reorder insertion line is drawn with ::before / ::after on the row wrapper
question: How to mark a drop point while reordering?
answer: With `::before` / `::after` on the row wrapper, never an inserted element, so marking a drop point never reflows the list out from under the pointer.
state: live

### D-5
date: 2026-09-06
milestone: M2
scope: extension
title: A duplicated item gets a ' copy' suffix
question: What does Duplicate name the copy?
answer: The copy lands directly under the original with every field and a ` copy` suffix on the name, so two items cannot resolve each other's drop placeholder (`waitForEvent` matches on name). Duplicate lives in the template panel. Rename is double-click → inline pre-selected input; Enter and blur commit, Escape cancels. Moving between calendars is a drop on another category's header or empty space; the category chips on the add form stayed.
state: live

### D-6
date: 2026-09-06
milestone: M5
scope: extension
title: Template fields are omitted from events.insert when unset, and reminderMin is checked against undefined
question: How do description, location, colorId and reminderMin map onto the event body?
answer: Straight onto the `events.insert` body, each omitted when unset: an empty `location` is a real value to the API, and sending `reminders` at all turns off the calendar's defaults. `reminderMin: 0` means "at start", so every check is against `undefined`, never falsy. No new scope, no new endpoint. The fields live in a per-item panel collapsed by default, opened from the row's chevron, which stays tinted while any field is set.
state: live

### D-7
date: 2026-09-06
milestone: M5
scope: extension
title: The drag preview and drop placeholder draw in the item's colorId
question: What colour does the drag preview use?
answer: The item's `colorId`, so what you see while dragging is the colour the event will be.
state: live

### D-8
date: 2026-09-06
milestone: M7
scope: publishing
title: OAuth scopes are narrowed to calendar.events.owned and calendar.calendarlist.readonly
question: Which Calendar scopes does the extension request, given store review and OAuth verification?
answer: `calendar.events.owned` (create events on calendars the user owns; it also grants read on them, which phase 6 will need) and `calendar.calendarlist.readonly` (group pool items by calendar), replacing the blanket `auth/calendar`. Calendars whose `accessRole` is not `owner` are filtered out of the sidebar, since they cannot be written under this scope. The consent screen in Google Cloud must list the new scopes, and an existing cached token may need re-consent.
state: live

### D-9
date: 2026-09-06
milestone: M5
scope: extension
title: Capturing an existing calendar event into the pool is removed
question: Keep the capture feature (drag an event onto the sidebar, or an Add to pool button in Google's event dialog)?
answer: Built (commits 009b2c4..1496596) and removed (4ab49c5). It cost a second API round trip and a DOM observer on Google's dialog markup — not worth that surface for a pool you fill once; typing an item is faster. Phase 5 has since given `PoolItem` the fields a capture would fill, so reviving it would be cheaper, but the DOM observer on Google's markup is still the reason not to.
state: live

<!-- emerald: inbox.format.md -->
---
format: inbox
title: Inbox
language: 22
claims: none
---

What another project's session hands this document: a fact it found, a finding about this project, a gate it knows of. Until M44 the only channel was the author's clipboard, and three hand-offs of six never arrived; a message to another session lasts only as long as that session. A row lasts as long as the document.

`from` is the sending document's path, absolute — `F:/projects/me/me.emd` — and never a session's title, because a title stops meaning anything when the session ends and a path is where the sender's own record can be read. `refs` is the sender's names for the same things in its own document, so the owner can ask that document rather than the session that wrote it.

The rules are a convention and not a guarantee: the engine does not know which session writes a row (D-138), so nothing here can enforce them. A sender writes a row once, with `state: new`, and never edits it. The owner moves `state` and fills what it became. A foreign session writes to no other format of this document.

What it became is three columns, because the engine names a link's target by its first text column and only some formats have a unique one: `answered_by` and `became_unit` are links, to a decision and a unit; `became` is text for everything else — an open question or a review entry by its ref, a rule or a term by its name, or why it was dropped.

A text .emd is rewritten whole on every write, so a row written from another project while a session here is writing is the concurrent-writers case and nothing new: the plugin's writers take the document's lock in turn, a batch applies whole or not at all, a copy is taken before it, and the app's save refuses to overwrite a change it has not seen. A session is told a row arrived by running `node dist-plugin/server.mjs watch <this document> --format inbox --match state=new` under its Monitor tool: one line per new row, naming its subject, and silence for every other write (M44 U3, D-441).

An inbox row makes no claim.

| column | type | points at | unit | per | required | values | min | max | note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| subject | text |  |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The hand-off in a line: what `start_here` lists and what the text .emd heads the row with. |
| from | text |  |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The sending document's absolute path — `F:/projects/me/me.emd`. Not a session's title: a title means nothing once the session ends. |
| sent_on | date |  |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The day it was sent. |
| body | text |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | The fact or finding in full, in markdown, written for a session that has none of the sender's context. |
| refs | text |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | The sender's own refs for it, in its own document — `me.emd D-10, D-18`. |
| state | enum |  |  |  | yes | new, triaged, applied, dropped |  |  | A sender writes `new`, and `start_here` lists those. `triaged`: the owner has read it and knows what it will become. `applied`: it became that. `dropped`: read and not acted on, and `became` says why. |
| answered_by | link | decision |  |  | no |  |  |  | The decision it became or answered. |
| became_unit | link | unit |  |  | no |  |  |  | The unit it became. |
| became | text |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | Anything else it became, by ref or name — an open question, a review entry, a rule, a term — or why it was dropped. |

## rows

### Usuals' privacy policy is live on kfireliyahu.com: Google's OAuth verification can start
from: F:/projects/kfireliyahu.com/site.emd
sent_on: 2026-10-12
