<!-- emerald: review_entry.format.md -->
---
format: review_entry
title: Review entry
language: 4
claims: none
---

What a unit session left for the author to drive: docs/review.md, which reached 1,214 lines and thirty entries with no way to ask which four were live. That file is frozen as of D-238 and this format is what sorts it.

`state` is the column whose absence made the graveyard, and it is the whole reason this format exists rather than the prose staying prose. Thirty entries with no state is a queue nobody can triage; thirty rows with one is four `match` calls. The finding review.md was built on still stands — four real documents, and nothing else, ever rewrote this design — and what failed was the deferral, not the writing down.

An entry is deliberately four prose columns rather than one body. `do`, `should`, `breaks` and `not_tried` are the four questions the file's own template asks, and separating them is what lets a session be handed `breaks` alone: "where nothing checked it" is the half that predicts the next expensive bug, and it was buried in the middle of a bullet list.

`unit` is required. An entry with no unit is a note, and this project already has somewhere to put notes.

A review entry makes no claim — which is gap #1 of the four D-238 predicted, met for the second time in this document.

| column | type | points at | unit | per | required | values | min | max | note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| unit | link | unit |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The unit this is about. Required: an entry with no unit is a note rather than something to drive. |
| do | text |  |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The screen, the route and the file to open, in order. Named by route and file, never by "the screen you added" — the reader has no memory of the work. |
| should | text |  |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The figure, the row, the message. Precise enough to be wrong, which is the only kind of claim a review can reject. |
| breaks | text |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | Where it breaks, usually wherever nothing checked it. Said plainly. This is the column worth reading on its own. |
| not_tried | text |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | What could not be tried in the session that built it, and why — a real document, a running model, a Mac. |
| state | enum |  |  |  | yes | open, accepted, fault, dropped |  |  | **The column whose absence made the graveyard.** `open` has not been driven or was outranked and still stands. `accepted` was driven and holds. `fault` was driven and is wrong, and work follows from it. `dropped` stopped mattering — usually because the milestone it belonged to stopped. |

## rows

### 1) npm run build, reload the unpacked extension, open Google Calendar with the hamburger nav OPEN; then in DevTools > Application > Extension storage (local) delete primaryCalendarName and reload. 2) Close the nav with the hamburger, delete primaryCalendarName again, reload, wait 5 s, then open the nav. 3) Reload once more with the nav closed (cache present). 4) Create an item named "חצי חינם" in the calendar "הפתוחה" and one named "workout"; hover each chip; double-click the Hebrew one to rename; open its details and type a Hebrew location and description; drag it over the week grid (with and without Shift) and drop it; press Undo. 5) Open the composer's calendar dropdown and pick "ימי הולדת"; fold and unfold the Hebrew sections; make a calendar empty to see it under "No items yet". 6) Set a display name on a calendar in Google's settings if possible (summaryOverride) and reload.
unit: M9 U7
should: 1) The primary section/option/details read "Kfir Raphael Eliyahu", never the account's email address, and primaryCalendarName is written to local storage. 2) With the nav closed it shows the address or the name (whichever the DOM allows) and switches to "Kfir Raphael Eliyahu" within about a second of the nav opening. 3) The name shows at load with no flash of the address beyond the calendar fetch. 4) The Hebrew chip has its name at the right edge with ", 1h" to its left and controls unfolding on the left; "workout" is unchanged (name left, controls right). The rename field, location and description start at the right. The drag preview and loading placeholder show the Hebrew name right-aligned; the tooltip reads "חצי חינם · Sun, Oct 11, 7:00 - 8:00 PM" with the name intact; the toasts read ""חצי חינם" added!" and ""חצי חינם" removed" in correct order. 5) Hebrew section titles and empty rows right-align and truncate with the ellipsis on the left; the closed calendar select shows the Hebrew name right-aligned with correct order; the options list renders RTL. 6) The overridden name appears everywhere.
breaks: The page matcher in src/names.ts (findPrimaryName): if Google's calendar checkboxes do not carry the calendar name as aria-label/aria-labelledby/<label>, or the Google Account button's aria-label is not in the top document or lacks the name, it silently falls back to the address. If the nav's list is removed from the DOM when closed, step 2 relies on the observer firing when it is re-added. Second most likely: the mirrored chip's hover controls and the duration select's width in RTL, and the right-aligned Hebrew name in the wide calendar select leaving a gap after the colour dot.
not_tried: Could not load the extension in Chrome or open the real Google Calendar page, so Google's actual DOM for "My calendars" and the account button is assumed, not observed; findPrimaryName was only exercised on a hand-made mock DOM (normal case, other account, list not found, no other calendars, unreadable primary entry). RTL rendering was checked on a mock page with approximate styles, not with content.css in the real sidebar. A Google Calendar UI in Hebrew (html dir=rtl), multiple signed-in accounts (/u/1) and Arabic names were not tried.
state: accepted

