### Hover an item, click its pencil (Edit details), change the colour, then type a location and pause, and type a description and click away.
unit: M8 U23
should: The pencil reads as Edit details. A quiet '✓ Saved' appears and fades after each change: at once for the colour, after a pause in typing or on leaving the field for text. The details still reach the event on the next drop.
state: open

### In Week view, hold Shift, then press on an item and drag it to a time slot; release with Shift still down. Repeat releasing Shift before letting go, press Escape mid-drag once, and do an ordinary drag without Shift.
unit: M8 U24
should: The Shift-first drag shows a grabbing cursor, the tooltip and a dashed ↻ preview, and creates a weekly series; releasing Shift first makes a single event; Escape cancels with nothing created; Google's quick-create dialog never opens. An ordinary drag's tooltip says 'Hold Shift to repeat weekly' until Shift goes down.
state: open

### Load the new build over an existing pool; rename an item in one tab or machine and watch another.
unit: M8 U1
should: The pool is intact after migration; the rename appears elsewhere within ~15 s.
not_tried: Over-quota fallback (>8 KB description) and two different Google accounts on one Chrome profile.
state: accepted

### On an item's duration select choose Custom… and type 1h20, then try 20, 1:20, 2.5h and an invalid value; do the same in the composer. Drag the 1h20 item onto the grid.
unit: M8 U25
should: Presets now run 15m to 4h; Custom… accepts the forms above rounded to 5 minutes (5m to 24h), Enter sets and Escape keeps the old value, invalid text is refused. The chip reads '1h 20m', and the drag preview, placeholder and created event all span 1h 20m.
state: open

### Pick an item with its calendar icon, click a time slot, double-click, Shift-click; Escape; then Tab to the icon, Enter, schedule from the form.
unit: M7 U2
should: Click-to-place creates the event and Google's quick-create never opens; with nothing picked Google's own clicks work as before; the keyboard form schedules and Escape closes it.
state: accepted

### Rebuild and reload the extension, open Google Calendar week view (light theme, then dark). 1) Tab to an item's calendar button, Enter: the form opens with focus on the Day field. Pick another day, Tab to Start, Tab to Repeat and choose the weekly option, press Enter. 2) Click the schedule button again, then Shift-click a time on the grid. 3) Open an existing item's details that had a reminder set before this change (e.g. '1 hour before' and 'At start'), and one with 'Default reminder'. 4) On the default one press 'Add notification', type 60 in the count, Tab to the unit, choose hours, then press ×. 5) Open the colour dot dropdown with the keyboard (Tab, Alt+Down or Space), pick Tomato, then 'Calendar colour'. 6) Drop the item onto the grid and open the created event.
unit: M9 U6
should: 1) The Repeat option reads 'Weekly on <weekday of the chosen day>' and changes when the day changes; Enter creates a weekly series on that weekday at the chosen time, the toast says 'repeats weekly', and focus returns to the schedule button. 2) Shift-click creates a weekly series even though the form says 'Does not repeat'. 3) '1 hour before' shows '1 | hours', 'At start' shows '0 | minutes', the default one shows 'Add notification'; opening details changes nothing in storage. 4) After Add, focus is in the count showing '30 | minutes'; typing 60 stays '60 | minutes' while typing (stored 60); choosing hours then shows '60 | hours' (stored 3600); × brings back 'Add notification' with keyboard focus on it, and the item then drops with the calendar's default reminders. 5) Every colour option is reachable by keyboard, the field shows only the dot and ▾ with a visible focus ring, the chip repaints immediately, and a screen reader announces 'Colour for <name>' and the colour's name. 6) The event has the item's location, description, colour and one popup reminder of exactly the shown amount. Everything is legible in both themes and nothing overflows the 280px rail.
breaks: The dot-only colour select (text painted transparent; forced-colours/high-contrast mode may show the text over the dot, or an odd width with Google Sans); NotificationRow's local count/unit resync (an item edited on another machine while the row is open, or blur after typing past 4 weeks); focus moves after Add notification / × (effect-based); Enter in the schedule form on a FieldSelect; the notification row's width with real Google Sans at 248px.
not_tried: Could not load the extension in Chrome on Google Calendar, so none of the live behaviour, live page colours, real font widths, screen-reader output or Shift paths were exercised. Checked only: tsc, vite build, a node script for the reminder round trip (every minute 0..40320 and the old presets) and the weekday wording, and a static HTML mock with the built CSS in light and dark showing the layout fits.
state: accepted

### Reload the extension and Google Calendar, week view, light theme, Google's menu closed. (1) Resize the window to about 1000, 700, 450 and 300 px tall, with the pool expanded and the composer open, then with the composer closed, then collapsed to the strip. (2) Make the window narrow enough that Google would scroll sideways and check the bottom edge. (3) With the pool expanded, click Google's ☰ to open its menu, wait, then close it; repeat with the pool already collapsed by you; then with the menu open click the strip's chevron to open the pool, then close the menu. (4) Reload with Google's menu left open. (5) Keyboard only: Tab to "Hide sidebar", Enter; Enter again on the strip's chevron. (6) Switch Google to Dark (Settings > Appearance) and repeat 1 and 3. (7) Optional: set Google's language to Hebrew and reload.
unit: M9 U8
should: (1) At every height the "About · Support ↗" footer is fully visible with its top divider and 12 px below the text; the header is 64 px; the list scrolls between them; at ~300 px the composer itself scrolls (thin scrollbar on hover) instead of pushing the footer out. The 40 px strip spans exactly top to bottom. No flashing scrollbar while the composer opens. (2) The page shows no horizontal scrollbar caused by the shift. (3) Opening ☰ turns the pool into the 40 px strip at the same time as Google's nav slides in, and the grid's width barely changes; closing ☰ brings the pool back expanded. If you had collapsed it yourself it stays collapsed. Opening the pool from the strip with the menu open keeps both open until you close the menu, after which the pool stays open. Nothing about this changes the collapsed state stored after a reload with the menu closed. (4) The pool loads straight as the strip, without first sliding to 280 px. (5) Focus lands on the strip's chevron after hiding and on "Hide sidebar" after showing, with a visible ring. (6) Same in dark. (7) Sidebar on the left, English in normal order, Google's layout filling the rest with no gap and no overlap.
breaks: Detection depends on [role=main] being the grid only: if Google's nav is inside it, the pool never steps aside (harmless, but the feature is dead). If Google animates its nav as an overlay at your window width, the pool also stays (by design). In RTL, if Google's root is positioned with a physical left:0 it would overlap the sidebar by 280 px. overflow-x: clip on Google's root could hide a popup that sticks out past the root's left edge (it was under the sidebar anyway). Repeated ☰ toggling during the 200 ms transition could leave the strip/expanded state one step behind until the next resize or click.
not_tried: Nothing was run in Chrome: the extension could not be loaded here, so the scrollbar diagnosis of the clipping, the nav geometry threshold (120 px), the transition feel, both themes, focus moves and the Hebrew right-to-left page are all untested. Only `npm run build` and `tsc --noEmit` were run, both clean.
state: accepted

### Reload the extension and the Google Calendar tab (Week view). In the sidebar footer click Export and open the downloaded usuals-<date>.json. Delete one usual in the sidebar and rename another, then click Import and choose that file. Import the same file again. Then edit the file: change one item's id, and give another a colorId of "99"; import it. Finally import any other .json file.
unit: M8 U18
should: Export downloads usuals-2026-10-11.json (today's date) holding every usual under its calendarId with the calendar's name, and the toast says "Exported N usuals". The first import brings back only the deleted usual, at the end of its calendar, and leaves the renamed one renamed: "Imported 1 usual". The second import says "Nothing new to import". The edited file adds the item with the new id as a copy, the colour-99 one without its colour. The unrelated file says "That file is not a Usuals export". After a reload everything imported is still there.
breaks: Whether a[download] on a blob made in the content script downloads with the given name on calendar.google.com, rather than opening the JSON in a tab. An import big enough to pass sync's 100 KB falls back to "saved on this device only", as any edit would.
not_tried: Not driven in Chrome; the file format's round trip and validation were run under node only.
state: accepted

### Reload the extension at chrome://extensions and reload the Google Calendar tab with DevTools open. Use the sidebar: drop an item, click-to-place one, collapse and reopen the sidebar, open and close Google's main menu, resize the window. Check the console shows no [Usuals] lines. Then run localStorage.setItem('usualsDebug', '1') in that console, reload, and repeat; then localStorage.removeItem('usualsDebug'). (Renamed from [WeeklyPool] and wlpDebug in d8326e0, D-27.)
unit: M7 U9
should: With the switch off, no [WeeklyPool] log lines at all during normal use. With it on, 'Sidebar injected.' at load, an 'adjustLayout: shifting' line on each collapse, menu toggle and resize, 'Calendar time zone' on each create, and a 'Place' or 'Drop' line with the datekey and date. Removing the key quiets them again at once, without a reload. Events are created exactly as before.
breaks: Any [WeeklyPool] console.log with the switch off; logs not returning with it on; any change in drop, place, collapse or layout behaviour.
not_tried: Not loaded in Chrome: only tsc, the build and a grep of dist/content.js and dist/background.js (the one console.log left is inside the gated helper). Not tried with site data blocked for calendar.google.com, where the switch reads as off.
state: accepted

### Reload the extension at chrome://extensions and reload the Google Calendar tab, in Week view with Google's main menu (☰) open. In Google's left list: create a calendar (⊕ next to Other calendars → Create new calendar, then back), recolour one from its ⋮ menu, rename one in its settings, and delete the new one. Then close Google's menu, recolour a calendar from Settings, and open the menu again. Also make a change in a second tab and come back after a minute.
unit: M8 U19
should: Each change shows in the sidebar about a second after Google's own list shows it, with no loading flash: a new calendar appears at the end under the calendars without items, a recolour changes its section's dot and its items' chips, a rename changes the heading, a deleted calendar's section disappears. Items, folded sections and the composer's chosen calendar stay as they were. With the menu closed nothing happens until it is opened again.
breaks: If Google's rows do not hold the checkbox inside the [data-id] element, the reading is always empty and nothing refreshes (only coming back to the tab after a minute does). A recolour that Google applies without touching a style attribute or the row's children would also be missed.
not_tried: Nothing driven on the live page: no signed-in Calendar was available to the session. Typecheck and build pass.
state: accepted

### Reload the extension at chrome://extensions and reload the Google Calendar tab. In Week view, type a name in the composer without adding it. Press d (Day), w (Week), m (Month), y (Year), a (Schedule), and pick a custom view from the view menu. Open Settings (gear) and come back. Hide weekends (view menu) and check Week again. Finally set the default view to Day in Settings and open calendar.google.com/calendar/r directly.
unit: M8 U17
should: The sidebar shows in Week (7 days or Mon–Fri) and Day, and is gone in Month, Year, Schedule, the custom view and Settings, where Google's grid fills the whole width with no 280 px gap. The half-typed name is still in the composer after returning. In Day view a drop and a click-to-place land at the slot aimed at, and the schedule form offers only that day. Picking an item and then pressing m un-picks it.
breaks: A bare /r reads the grid, so it shows for a moment late or not at all if Google draws the grid slowly; a custom 5- or 7-day view starting on the week's first day reads as a week there. A drag in progress when the view changes by keyboard was not handled beyond clearing the tooltip.
not_tried: Nothing was driven in a real Google Calendar tab: the session had no signed-in Calendar to load the unpacked extension into. Whether Chrome's Navigation API fires currententrychange for Google's pushState in a content script is unverified; the 500 ms check covers it either way.
state: accepted

### Reload the extension. Calendar Settings → General → Time zone: (GMT-04:00) New York, Windows stays on Israel time; reload the Calendar tab. Week view: gutter reads GMT-04. Drop, click-to-place and keyboard-form an item at 10:00; Shift-drop one at 10:00 on Sun Oct 25. Then set the zone back to Jerusalem and drop at 10:00.
unit: M8 U14
should: Console logs `Calendar time zone → {kind:'iana', id:'America/New_York'} …T14:00:00.000Z`; each event shows 10:00–11:00 on the grid (17:00 Israel); the form's Today and default time follow New York; the series stays at 10:00 across both DST changes. Back on Jerusalem: `{kind:'iana', id:'Asia/Jerusalem'}` and the same event as before the fix.
breaks: If the gutter label is not found on the real DOM, the primary zone is still used — correct unless the primary calendar's zone differs from the display zone.
not_tried: Not run in Chrome yet.
state: open

### Remove and reinstall the unpacked extension; then in Google Calendar switch to Month, Year and Schedule view, click Open Week view, and later dismiss the pill with ×. Also open Settings.
unit: M8 U22
should: Install opens Calendar in Week view once. In Month/Year/Schedule a small pill at the bottom left offers Open Week view for the same date, in the page's light or dark theme, without moving Google's layout; × hides it for good, also after reload. No pill in Week, Day or Settings.
state: open

### Run `npm run build`, reload the extension at chrome://extensions, then reload Google Calendar in week view with Google's hamburger menu closed. In DevTools, select the `#weekly-life-pool-sidebar` element and check its inline style for `--wlp-page`. Compare the sidebar's background with Google's top bar right next to it. Hover the category list, then move the pointer away, then Tab into an item row. Hover the collapse chevron. Switch Google Calendar to Dark (Settings > Appearance) and repeat without reloading. Collapse the sidebar to the 40 px strip in both themes.
unit: M9 U2
should: `--wlp-page` is set (an rgb() value, about rgb(248, 250, 253) in light). The sidebar and Google's top bar look like one continuous colour, with no line at the sidebar's right edge, in light and in dark (after a dark switch the colour changes within about 3 s, no reload). Text is Google Sans; calendar titles are 16 px medium in normal case (not capitals); item rows are 32 px tall, 4 px apart, names 14 px. The list shows a thin scrollbar thumb only while hovered or while focus is inside it, and no grey track. The chevron's hover is a visible grey circle. In dark the + New item pill is a slightly lighter grey than the sidebar. The collapsed strip uses the same background as the expanded sidebar. Tab still reaches the pill, the chevron and every row with a visible focus ring.
breaks: The surface read: if Google has no `[role="banner"]`, or its header and ancestors are transparent or semi-transparent, `--wlp-page` is absent and the constants (#f8fafd / #1f1f1f) stand in, which may not match the dark page exactly. Long calendar names at 16 px now truncate with an ellipsis. Hover on the schedule/details panel buttons is now translucent over the panel. Nothing checked the thin scrollbar's actual width on Windows or whether Chrome repaints scrollbar-color on hover.
not_tried: Could not load the extension into Chrome or open Google Calendar from this session, so nothing visual was seen: the sampled colour, both themes, the strip, scrollbar and type sizes are all unobserved. Only `npm run build` and `tsc --noEmit` were run (both clean).
state: accepted

### Run npm run build, reload the extension at chrome://extensions, reload Google Calendar in week view. Look at the sidebar header; click "+ New item" twice; hover it. Tab from the page into the sidebar until the pill and then the chevron are focused, and press Enter/Space on each. Switch Google Calendar to Dark (Settings > Appearance) and repeat. Collapse with the chevron, check the 40 px strip, expand again, then reload the page with the form closed.
unit: M9 U1
should: No "Weekly Pool" title or calendar icon. Header is the same height as Google's top bar (bottom edges line up, ~64 px). Left: a raised rounded-rectangle button with a plus and "New item" in Google Sans, white with a soft shadow in light, a grey a step lighter than the sidebar in dark, looking like Google's own "Create" next to it; hover deepens the shadow. First click hides the add form and the plus is a plus; next click shows it, focuses the "New item…" input, and the plus has turned into a cross. Both controls show a blue focus ring when tabbed to and act on Enter/Space; a screen reader reads "New item, button, expanded/collapsed". Chevron collapses to the 40 px strip whose button expands again. Form open/closed survives a reload.
breaks: Google's global button styles beating the !important resets (font, padding or background of the pill wrong); the dark pill colour #37393b not matching Google's real dark Create; the shadow looking too heavy or clipped by the sidebar's overflow:hidden at the top/left edge; the focus ring's 2 px offset clipped at the header's left padding.
not_tried: Could not load the extension in Chrome or open Google Calendar from this session, so nothing was seen rendered in either theme; checked only that tsc is clean and the build emits the new classes. No screen reader run.
state: accepted

### Shift-drop an item onto Week view; then Undo from the toast; then a plain drop and Undo.
unit: M3 U1
should: A weekly series with no end at the dropped time; Undo on it removes every instance ("Weekly series … removed"); a plain drop's Undo removes only that event.
state: accepted

### Week view, drop an item; then in DevTools on calendar.google.com: `$$('[data-eventid]').map(e => atob(e.dataset.eventid.replace(/-/g,'+').replace(/_/g,'/')))`
unit: M8 U3
should: The placeholder fades as soon as the chip renders. `data-eventid` decodes to `<eventId>_<YYYYMMDD> <calendar email>` (base64url, no padding) — the instance form `waitForEvent` handles.
not_tried: Day view and Month view chips were not sampled.
state: accepted

### npm run build, reload the unpacked extension at chrome://extensions. Before reloading Google Calendar, if an older build left the form closed, keep it that way (or run chrome.storage.local.set({formOpen:false}) from the extension's service-worker console). Reload Google Calendar in week view, Light, without clicking anything. 1) Look at the sidebar's top band beside Google's top bar. 2) Press a key Google uses (e.g. 'w', 'm') straight after load. 3) Type a long title (~30 characters) and a Hebrew one. 4) Tab from the title: Add, chevron, calendar, duration, then the list. 5) Click an empty calendar's row under "No items yet". 6) Collapse with the chevron using Enter, then expand from the strip with Enter. 7) Shrink the window to ~300 px tall. 8) Reload while the calendars are loading (or with the network offline for the error state). 9) Switch Google Calendar to Dark and look again.
unit: M9 U11
should: No "+ New item" button. The top band is the composer's first row: "Add title" on its underline, the blue Add pill, then the round chevron at the right edge, all vertically centred in a band whose bottom lines up with Google's top bar (64 px); the calendar/duration row and the hint sit under it, then the divider. The composer shows even though an old build stored it closed, and chrome.storage.local no longer has a formOpen key. Nothing in the sidebar takes focus on load, so Google's keyboard shortcuts work at once. Long titles scroll inside the field without pushing Add or the chevron; Hebrew starts at the right. Tab order follows the reading order above. The empty-calendar row selects that calendar (dot and name) and puts the caret in the title. Collapsing moves focus to the strip's chevron, expanding moves it back to the header's chevron. In a short window the header and footer stay whole and the calendar row and hint scroll inside the composer. While loading or on error the header shows only the chevron, at the right. Chips still lift with a shadow on hover, and Add gets its shadow on hover, in both themes.
breaks: The title field is only ~145 px wide beside Add and the chevron, so typed titles may feel cramped; the 40 px title field may sit a pixel or two off Google's top bar centre line; Google's global button styles beating .wlp-header-hide's margin so the lone chevron sits on the left while loading; the chevron between Add and the calendar field in tab order may feel odd to keyboard users; .wlp-composer as a flex column scroll container squashing its rows instead of scrolling; the renamed --wlp-shadow missing somewhere and leaving the chip/Add hover flat.
not_tried: Nothing was seen rendered: the extension cannot be loaded into Chrome from this session. Only npx tsc --noEmit and npm run build were run (both clean) and the built CSS was checked for the new classes. The formOpen key removal, focus handling, short-window scrolling, both themes and screen-reader output are untested.
state: accepted

### npm run build, reload the unpacked extension, open Google Calendar in Light. 1) Click "+ New item" to open the form. 2) Look at it beside Google's own event editor (open any event > More options). 3) Tab from the title: Add (if text typed), calendar dropdown, duration dropdown; on the calendar dropdown press Down/Up, type a calendar's first letter, Alt+Down to open, Escape to close. 4) Type "test" and press Enter; type "test2" and press Shift+Enter. 5) Pick a calendar with a long name. 6) Click an empty calendar's row under "No items yet". 7) Switch Google Calendar to Dark (Settings > Appearance) and repeat 1-2.
unit: M9 U5
should: Row 1: "Add title" in 18px on a thin grey underline that turns into a 2px blue line while focused, with a blue "Add" pill to its right (greyed and unclickable while the title is empty). Row 2: a grey field with the selected calendar's round colour dot, its name and a ▾ caret, filling the row, then a grey "1h ▾" field. No chip cloud anywhere. Keyboard: arrows change the calendar and the dot follows; Escape closes the open list and focus stays on the select; a focus ring shows on each select. Enter and Shift+Enter both add the item to the chosen calendar and clear the title with focus kept in it. A long name ends in an ellipsis inside the field, never pushing the duration off the row. The empty-calendar row opens the form with that calendar selected and focus in the title. In Dark, fields are a grey one step lighter than the page (~#2f3133), text light, Add a light-blue pill with dark text.
breaks: Google's global input/select/button styles beating a rule (a native arrow or border showing, wrong font); the calendar select not shrinking (min-width) so the duration field overflows; field-sizing:content on the duration select being ignored and the field taking its widest option; the --wlp-field colour-mix declared only in the light block resolving with light values in Dark; Lightning CSS's color-mix fallback blocks reordering custom properties.
not_tried: Nothing was seen rendered: the extension cannot be loaded into Chrome from this session, and serving a static mock on localhost was refused by the permission system, so layout, both themes, ellipsis, focus rings and screen-reader announcements are untested. Only tsc --noEmit and npm run build were run (both clean).
state: accepted

### npm run build, reload the unpacked extension, open Google Calendar week view (light theme). 1) Look at the list. 2) Click a section's title row (e.g. Work); click it again. 3) Tab to a section title and press Enter/Space. 4) Fold a section, then drag an item from another section onto its title row and drop. 5) Click a row under "No items yet" (e.g. Gaming), type a name, Enter. 6) Drag the only item out of a section into another section. 7) On a second computer or tab, check the fold. 8) Switch Google Calendar to dark theme and repeat 1-2.
unit: M9 U3
should: 1) Calendars with items are sections: coloured rounded square, name at 16 px sentence case, chevron pointing up at the right; no eye icon, no "Hidden" strip; empty calendars appear once at the bottom under a single "No items yet" caption as grey rows. Calendars hidden before this update show as folded sections (chevron down, item count). 2) Items disappear and the chevron turns down with a count; second click restores them. 3) Same via keyboard; focus ring visible on the row; screen reader says "<calendar name>, expanded/collapsed, button". 4) The folded title row lights up blue while aimed at; on drop the item is at the top of that section and the section is unfolded. 5) The add form opens (if closed) with Gaming's chip selected and the cursor in the name field; after Enter, Gaming moves up into its own section, unfolded, with the item. 6) The emptied calendar moves down into the "No items yet" rows. 7) The fold matches. 8) Text, hover and drop highlight all readable on the dark surface.
breaks: Google's global stylesheet overriding the h3/button resets (title row too tall, wrong font, a default button background); the chevron rotation selector; drop highlight on the folded header being covered by the toggle's hover; focus not landing in the name input after clicking an empty row while the form animates open; the section jumping position when it gets its first item or loses its last, which may feel disorienting mid-drag.
not_tried: Could not load the extension in Chrome, so nothing was seen rendered in either theme, no drag was performed, and no screen reader was run; only npm run build and tsc --noEmit were checked. Cross-device sync of the fold was not tested.
state: accepted

### npm run build, reload the unpacked extension, open Google Calendar week view in Light. 1) Look at a section with items, including one item given Peacock or Tangerine in its details. 2) Hover a chip, then move away. 3) Click in the page, then Tab into the list until focus reaches an item's duration, Tab on to its calendar icon, press Enter, then Escape. 4) Drag a chip a little and look at the image under the pointer, then over the week grid with and without Shift, and drop. 5) Double-click a name, type, Enter. 6) Drag a chip above another, and onto another calendar's heading. 7) Open details, Duplicate. 8) Switch Google Calendar to Dark (Settings > Appearance) and repeat 1-4.
unit: M9 U4
should: 1) Each item is a filled chip in its event colour with 4px corners, 12px text reading "name, 1h" (name medium, duration regular), no controls, text readable on every colour (dark text on Peacock/Tangerine/pastels, white on Tomato/Grape/Blueberry/Basil/Graphite). 2) A soft shadow and the calendar, chevron and x icons appear on hover in the chip's text colour; they vanish on leave and the name widens again. 3) The icons appear as soon as focus is in the row; each icon shows an inset ring in the chip's text colour when focused; Enter opens the schedule form with the icon still visible and the chip ringed blue; Escape returns focus to the icon. 4) The drag image is the chip without icons; over the grid the preview is a solid chip of the same colour with the name and a shadow; Shift dashes its border and adds the repeat glyph; the drop pulses a flat chip of the same colour and the event appears. 5) The rename field sits in the chip in its text colour with an underline and commits. 6) The blue drop line shows between chips and the move lands. 7) A copy appears under the original as a chip. 8) Same results on the dark page; chips keep their colours.
breaks: The drag image (an off-screen clone passed to setDragImage, removed on the next tick) may come out blank or show the default ghost; field-sizing:content on the duration select may not apply, leaving a gap after the duration; the API's calendar backgroundColor may not equal the colour Google paints the calendar's events, so a chip may not match its grid events exactly, most likely in the dark theme where Google may tint event colours; the ring of the picked chip touches neighbouring chips in the 4px gap.
not_tried: Nothing ran in Chrome: no extension load, so neither theme, the hover/focus reveal, the drag image, the grid preview, the placeholder or any of the flows were seen. Only npx tsc --noEmit and npm run build were run, and contrast was computed in Node for Google's 11 event colours and some calendar colours.
state: accepted

### npm run build, reload the unpacked extension, reload Google Calendar in Week view (Light), DevTools console open, at least two calendars with pool items and one without. 1) With ☰ closed, Tab to the sidebar footer's "Dock in Google's menu (experiment)" and press Enter. 2) Open ☰. 3) Close ☰, open it again. 4) Untick and re-tick a pool calendar's checkbox in Google's "My calendars", then tick/untick several quickly. 5) Drag a nested chip onto the week grid and drop; another with Shift held; Undo one from the toast (under the list). 6) Drag a chip onto another calendar's nested list, then onto the Google row of a calendar that has no items. Reorder within one list. 7) Fold and unfold a calendar's items with its count button; check the sidebar after closing ☰. 8) By keyboard: Tab to a chip, open its calendar icon with Enter (schedule popover), Escape; open details with Enter, Tab past the last field; open "+ New item" with Enter, add an item to a calendar without items, Escape. Click outside an open popover. 9) With an item picked, click a time on the grid. 10) Double-click a nested chip to rename. 11) Switch weeks, switch to Day and Month view and back, open Settings (gear) and come back, fold Google's own "My calendars" and unfold it. 12) Switch Google Calendar to Dark (Settings > Appearance) and back with ☰ open. 13) Reload with ☰ open. 14) Press the switch again under the list.
unit: M9 U10
should: 1) The button turns blue with a ✓, focus stays on it; nothing else changes while ☰ is closed. 2) The separate sidebar disappears (no 40 px strip), the grid takes the full width beside Google's nav; under each pool calendar with items, indented in line with its checkbox, a small "N items ˄" control and its chips (about 200 px wide, names ellipsized, duration whole); nothing under calendars without items; after the list, before "Other calendars", "+ New item", and the switch; console shows "[WeeklyPool] dock: under N calendar row(s)" once. 3) Closing ☰ brings the sidebar back exactly as before the experiment; reopening docks again with no strip flash. 4) Items stay under their own rows with no visible flicker (at most a "put back (redraw n)" line per change, n rising slowly); no "giving up". 5) Preview, tooltip, placeholder and event creation as from the sidebar; Shift makes it weekly; toast shows under the list and Undo works. 6) The chip moves to that calendar (top of its list); a calendar that had no items gets its own nested list; reorder lands where the line showed. 7) Folding leaves only the count; the sidebar's section for that calendar is folded too. 8) Each popover opens beside the chip/button with focus inside; Escape and Tab-out close it and put focus back on the control that opened it; the new item appears under its calendar's row; outside clicks close it. 9) The event is placed and the popover closes. 10) Rename saves. 11) Items stay under their rows; when "My calendars" is folded the separate sidebar shows instead and the nested items come back on unfold. 12) Colours switch with the page and stay readable, popover too. 13) It docks again after load. 14) The pool returns to the separate sidebar at once, the button shows no tick, still off after a reload. No console errors at any step.
breaks: Most likely: nothing docks because Google's calendar rows are not found with confidence (row taller than 64 px, the checkbox's direct parent holds other checkboxes, or the primary calendar's name is still the address) — console says "calendar rows not found". Next: Google's renderer removes foreign siblings on every draw so it gives up ("taken out 9 times in a row") or flickers; a virtualized or absolutely positioned list making our containers overlap rows; a duplicate-named calendar (e.g. one in "Other calendars" with a pool calendar's name) silently keeping the sidebar; the indent or ~200 px width too tight; drags starting in the nav blocked or a blank drag image; row drops not reaching our document listener; the popover placed over the wrong spot or behind Google's layers; Google's shortcuts or row handlers no longer firing for clicks inside our containers (they stop propagation); screen readers announcing the list's count oddly with our extra items.
not_tried: Nothing ran in Chrome against Google Calendar: no extension load, so the anchoring against Google's real nav, its re-render behaviour, drag from the nav, row drops, the popover's position and focus handling, both themes, RTL pages, a narrow window where Google overlays its nav, and screen-reader output were not seen. Ran: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json, npm run build, and 26 checks of dock.ts against a hand-made mock nav in Chromium with a virtual clock and manual frames (anchoring, duplicate/missing rules, li/listitem/div rows, redraw and move re-placement, refresh, hidden list fallback, fight give-up, spaced redraws).
state: dropped

### npm run build, reload the unpacked extension, reload Google Calendar in Week view (Light), DevTools console open. 1) With Google's menu (☰) closed, look at the sidebar footer, Tab to "Dock in Google's menu (experiment)" and press Enter. 2) Open ☰. 3) Close ☰, open it again. 4) In the docked Pool section, drag a chip onto the week grid and drop; drag another with Shift held; Undo one from the toast. 5) Click an item's calendar icon, click a time on the grid; pick again and use the schedule form's Schedule button. 6) Open an item's details, change the notification and colour; double-click to rename; drag an item onto another calendar's title row. 7) Fold and unfold the Pool title row, use its + to open the form and add an item. 8) Switch weeks, switch to Day and Month view and back, open Settings (gear) and come back, fold Google's own "My calendars". 9) Switch Google Calendar to Dark (Settings > Appearance) and back with ☰ open. 10) Reload the page with ☰ open. 11) Press the switch again in the docked footer.
unit: M9 U10
should: 1) The button turns blue with a ✓ and focus stays on it; nothing else changes while ☰ is closed. 2) The separate sidebar disappears entirely (no 40 px strip), the grid takes the full width beside Google's nav, and a "Pool" row with a chevron and + appears right after Google's "My calendars" list, above "Other calendars", with the calendar sections and chips under it; console shows "[WeeklyPool] dock: under \"My calendars\"" once. 3) Closing ☰ brings the sidebar back exactly as before the experiment (user's own collapsed choice, page shifted right); reopening docks again, no strip flash. 4) Preview, tooltip, placeholder and event creation work as from the sidebar; Shift makes it weekly; the toast shows inside the Pool section and Undo works. 5) Both place the event; the picked chip is ringed. 6) Edits save; the details' notification row fits on one line without a horizontal scrollbar; move lands. 7) Folding hides everything but the Pool row; the form opens under it. 8) The Pool section stays under "My calendars" (at most a "put back (n)" line per change); if Google folds "My calendars" or the route has no list, the sidebar shows instead and the Pool returns when the list does. 9) Colours switch with the page and stay readable. 10) It docks again after load. 11) The pool returns to the separate sidebar at once, the button shows no tick, and after a reload it is still off. No console errors at any step.
breaks: Most likely: the dock never appears because Google's "My calendars" title row has no aria-expanded button (or it has aria-haspopup), or the climb meets the mini month first — then the sidebar just stays and the console says "not found". Next: Google's nav re-render removing the foreign node so often it gives up ("giving up for this page"); the section placed inside "My calendars" (after the list rather than after the section) so Google's fold hides it; a drag from inside the nav not starting or the drag image coming out blank (the off-screen clone sits inside Google's nav, which may clip or transform it); Google keyboard shortcuts or nav handlers no longer firing on clicks inside the Pool because the container stops propagation; the composer, schedule form or details too wide for the nav and clipped; focus lost when the switch moves the pool.
not_tried: Nothing ran in Chrome: no extension load, so the anchor search against Google's real nav, the re-attach behaviour, drag from inside the nav, the fit at the nav's real width, both themes, RTL pages, a narrow window where Google overlays its nav, and screen-reader output were not seen. Only npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json and npm run build were run.
state: dropped

<!-- emerald: unit.format.md -->
---
format: unit
title: Unit
language: 14
claims: none
---

One unit of work, as docs/07-roadmap.md writes it: a checkbox, a clause, a date and the decision it produced. The roadmap is the plan of record and it is read end to end far too often — twenty-eight times, measured — because a session that wants to know what U4 was has no way to ask for one line.

A unit is where the project's own cadence lives: one unit per session, then commit and start fresh. So the questions this format has to answer are "which units of M20 are built", "what landed on this date" and "which decision came out of this one", and they are three `match` calls rather than three greps.

`decision` is optional and that is a real state rather than missing data: a unit that was dropped before it was built produced no decision, and a unit still open has not produced one yet. `landed` is empty for the same reason.

`ref` carries its milestone — `M20 U0` rather than `U0` — because a unit id is only unique inside its milestone and a link printed on another format's row has to be readable on its own. `milestone` is kept beside it anyway, because that is what gets filtered on.

`milestone` links to a row of `milestone` and a text document writes it as that row's `ref` (M27 U1); `match:{milestone:M20}` finds it by that name (M27 U2). `files` is what a unit touches, written when it is planned and corrected when it lands, and it replaced the `Files:` line a brief used to end with. `undone_by` is the decision that reversed a unit after it was built, so work undone is counted rather than reconstructed (D-286). `blocks` is the units it waits on, a link to several rows (M27 U2). `in_progress`, `taken_by` and `started` are a session at work on it (M27 U6): a session takes a unit with `update_format_rows`, addressing it by `ref` and expecting it still `planned`, so two that try at once cannot both get it.

A unit makes no claim.

| column | type | points at | unit | per | required | unique | values | min | max | note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ref | text |  |  |  | yes | yes |  |  |  | The unit as the roadmap names it, with its milestone — `M20 U0`, `M20 T1`. Unique on its own, which `U0` is not. |
| milestone | link | milestone |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | `M20` — the `milestone` row it belongs to, written by its `ref`. What gets filtered on, which is why it is a column of its own as well as part of `ref`; `match` takes the name as well as the id. |
| scope | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | `engine`, or the pack the unit belongs to — `ledger`. So `match:{state:planned, scope:engine}` is the engine's handoff and a parked pack's planned units never show up in it. |
| title | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | The clause the roadmap gives it, verbatim — `the pack is staged, and the rail is the engine's`. |
| landed | date |  |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | The day it was committed. Empty for a unit that is open or was dropped, which is a state rather than a missing figure. |
| decision | link | decision |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | The decision this unit produced. Empty for a unit that produced none — dropped before it was built, or not built yet. |
| state | enum |  |  |  | yes | no | planned, in_progress, built, dropped |  |  | `built` is committed and in the roadmap as ticked. `in_progress` is a session working on it now, set when it is handed out and moved on when it lands (M27 U6). `dropped` was scoped and then deliberately not built — M20's tail has seven of those and they are the reason this column is not a boolean. |
| brief | text |  |  |  | no | no |  |  |  |  |
| files | text |  |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | What the unit touches: written when it is planned and corrected when it lands (D-286). It replaces the `Files:` line a brief used to end with. |
| undone_by | link | decision |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | The decision that reversed this unit after it was built — M25 U4 by D-283 — so work undone is counted rather than reconstructed from transcripts (D-286). Empty for work that stands. |
| blocks | links | unit |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | The units that have to be built before this one can be — M27 U3 waiting on U2 (M27 U2, D-300). Several, in order, and empty for a unit nothing holds up. A unit is ready when every one of these is built. |
| taken_by | text |  |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | Who took it — the driver or the session that set it `in_progress` (M27 U6). Kept after it lands, so a unit says who built it. Called `claimed_by` until D-420: a format's band says Claims, and one word meant two things on one screen. |
| started | date |  |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | The day it went `in_progress` (M27 U6). Shown beside the state, because a session that dies leaves its unit `in_progress` for ever and only the date says so. |

