### M7 U11
milestone: M7
scope: publishing
title: OAuth verification: scope justifications and a demo video
landed: 2026-10-17
decision: D-34
state: built
brief: "Submit Google's OAuth verification for the calendar-usuals project (D-34). **Submitted by the author on 2026-10-15; approved by 2026-10-17 (D-35)** for `calendar.events.owned`.\n\nJustifications as submitted, drafted 2026-10-14 from src/background.ts:\n- calendar.calendarlist.readonly: Usuals shows the user's saved usual events in a panel beside Google Calendar, grouped under the user's own calendars with each calendar's name and colour. It reads the calendar list (calendarList.list) only to show those groups and to let the user choose which calendar a usual belongs to. It does not read events or calendar contents.\n- calendar.events.owned: When the user drags a usual onto a time, or schedules it with the day and time form, Usuals creates that one event (events.insert) in the calendar the user chose, which must be one they own. If the user clicks Undo right after, it deletes that same event (events.delete). It never reads, changes or deletes any other event. events.owned is the narrowest scope that allows creating events in the user's own calendars.\n\nThe demo video as made: a first plan of ten captioned shots was more than Google asks, so the author recorded a simpler one: 55 s, silent, no captions (Captura, 2026-10-15). It shows the store page and install; the extension's manifest at chrome-extension://hkihoifbohighgoackpcinobeabpjnbp/manifest.json with `oauth2.client_id` highlighted at about 0:08; Google Calendar opening the sign-in popup by itself, the account chooser, the unverified-app warning and the consent screen naming Usuals with both permissions ticked; the panel showing the account's calendar; a usual added, dragged onto the week, opened in Google's own event view, another placed and undone, and the Schedule form. The demo account was a new Gmail made for it, in a fresh Chrome profile. The popup's address bar shows accounts.google.com but cuts the URL before client_id, so the manifest shot is the one that shows it. Uploaded to YouTube as Unlisted on the demo Gmail account's channel, because YouTube is switched off for me@kfireliyahu.com; its link is not recorded here.\n\nTo get the consent screen again on an account that has already granted access: revoke Usuals at myaccount.google.com/connections and reload Calendar (authedFetch drops the revoked token and asks again), or revoke it at chrome://identity-internals. Uninstalling and reinstalling the extension does not, since the grant lives in the Google account.\n\n**Checked 2026-10-17, answering D-35's open points:** Google Auth Platform → Audience reads publishing status **In production**, user type **External**. So Testing mode did not cause the friend's request-access screen of 2026-10-16; the likelier cause is a work or school account whose admin restricts apps (or blocks unverified ones, which verification may now have cleared). Not yet retried. The same page's OAuth user cap reads **2 users / 100**: Google accounts that granted access while a requested scope was unapproved, counted for the project's lifetime and never reset. Google says the cap does not apply once every sensitive or restricted scope requested is approved, and that an \"unverified app\" screen means a requested scope is still unapproved. The author's fresh sign-in after approval showed no such screen, so `calendar.calendarlist.readonly` needs no approval of its own (it is not sensitive); nothing more to submit. The 2 counts Google accounts; the store's 4 users count Chrome installs."
taken_by: the author, with a Claude Code session drafting
started: 2026-10-15

