brief: 864530b. Calendars show `summaryOverride || summary` (calendarList has no fields mask, so the override already came back). The primary calendar, when its summary is still its address and it has no override, gets its name from the page (new src/names.ts, no new scope): a checkbox in Google's calendar list that comes before the first of the user's other calendars (by accessible name: aria-label, aria-labelledby or <label>) and whose name also appears in a page label carrying the account's address (the Google Account button), not equal to another calendar's name; exactly one must survive (a shorter name inside a longer one is dropped), otherwise the address stays. If none of the other calendars' names is found among the checkboxes, nothing is read. While unfound, a MutationObserver on body re-checks at most once a second and disconnects once found; the name is cached in chrome.storage.local (primaryCalendarName, keyed by calendar id) and shown at once on the next load, replaced by a fresh reading. RTL: dir="auto" on section titles, empty-calendar rows, composer title input and calendar <option>s (Chrome carries the option's direction to the closed select), details calendar/location/description, rename input, item name span, drag preview; the chip row gets dir from textDirection(name) so a Hebrew chip mirrors (name at right, ", 1h" to its left, controls unfold left) — chip CSS made logical. Toasts and the drop tooltip isolate the name with FSI/PDI instead of dir=auto, since they are English sentences. Design choice: calendar-name labels follow the brief and right-align (text-align:start) rather than staying left beside the dot as Google's own nav does. Unverified: the matcher against Google's real page (that "My calendars" checkboxes carry the calendar name as accessible name, that the account button's aria-label holds name and address in the main document, whether the list stays in the DOM with the nav closed); the extension was not loaded in Chrome. Logic checked on a mock DOM; RTL rendering of select/chip/ellipsis checked on a mock page. A Hebrew-UI Google page (html dir=rtl) was not considered.
